------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Presentations of groups
--
-- Structurally equivalent circuits over {H, S, CZ} have the same
-- path-sum, and the same isometry restriction, up to renaming the path
-- variables (Amy, QPL 2018, remark 2.8 and section 4.1)
--
-- PathSum.CRK.Structural proves remark 2.8's "circuits which are
-- equivalent up to symmetric monoidal laws are strictly equal in the
-- path-sum picture" for circuits over {H, CNOT, R_k, R_k†}.  This
-- module proves it for the Clifford gate set {H, S, CZ} of
-- PathSum.Circuit, the one corollary 4.4's development is built on,
-- and for both of the path-sums PathSum.Circuit gives a circuit:
-- definition 2.9's ⟦ C ⟧, in which every Hadamard allocates a path
-- variable, and the isometry restriction ⟦ C ⟧ᴿ of section 4.1, in
-- which a Hadamard allocates one only when a later Hadamard acts on
-- its wire, the last one putting the input x_w there instead.
--
-- Structural equivalence C ∼ C′ is, as there, the congruence on gate
-- lists generated by exchanging adjacent gates on disjoint wires
-- (Disjoint: no wire of one is a wire of the other; CZ acts on both of
-- its wires), closed under adding a gate in front (∼-cons) and so
-- under concatenation (∼-++); circuits on disjoint wires commute as
-- blocks (∼-parallel), the interchange law.
--
-- The theorems.  If C ∼ C′ then
--
--    ⟦ C ⟧  ≡ᴿ⟨ π ⟩ ⟦ C′ ⟧    (structural)    and
--    ⟦ C ⟧ᴿ ≡ᴿ⟨ π′ ⟩ ⟦ C′ ⟧ᴿ  (structuralᴿ),
--
-- for renamings π, π′ of the path variables read off the derivation:
-- the same normalisation, and every coefficient of the phase and of
-- every output equal, as an integer, once the path variables are
-- renamed (PathSum.Permute).  Hence the congruent forms (structural-≈,
-- structuralᴿ-≈) and ⟦ C ⟧ ≋ ⟦ C′ ⟧, ⟦ C ⟧ᴿ ≋ ⟦ C′ ⟧ᴿ (structural-≋,
-- structuralᴿ-≋).  The renaming is needed (renaming-needed,
-- renaming-needed-≈: H on wire 0 then on wire 1, against the other
-- order, differ at the coefficient of x₀ y₀, by ½, which is not 0
-- modulo 2^M).
--
-- The restriction too, because which Hadamards allocate is invariant:
-- a Hadamard allocates exactly when a later Hadamard acts on its wire,
-- and exchanging gates on disjoint wires changes, for no Hadamard,
-- whether a later one acts on its wire (hasH-∼); so the two circuits
-- have the same allocating Hadamards (allocs-∼), and exchanging two
-- that both allocate exchanges their path variables while any other
-- exchange renames nothing.
--
-- How the proofs go: as in PathSum.CRK.Structural, path by path, with
-- PathSum.Circuit.Trace's traces.  Two gates on disjoint wires update
-- a configuration alike in either order (gate-comm: a Hadamard reads
-- and writes only its wire, S and CZ change only the phase, reading
-- their wires); a derivation of C ∼ C′ gives a bijection of the bit
-- positions (PathSum.CRK.Structural's Reorder) under which the traces
-- agree (reorders, reordersᴿ); it restricts to a bijection of the path
-- variables (PathSum.CRK.Structural.toPerm), along which the phases
-- take the same values and the outputs the same bits; Möbius
-- uniqueness (PathSum.Permute.≡ᴿ-by-values) gives the coefficients.
-- No polynomial is computed or compared.
--
-- As for {H, CNOT, R_k}, "strictly equal" means: equal up to the
-- renaming of the bound path variables (α-conversion), coefficient by
-- coefficient (no function extensionality), with the normalisations
-- and the numbers of path variables equal as numbers.  Other sound
-- commutations (two phase gates on a shared wire, for instance) are
-- not monoidal laws and are not included.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

